[17592186051039 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186157334 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051039 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186157335 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051039 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186157347 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051039 #edb/ref 1031 "The Area of a 30-60-90 Triangle" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051039 #edb/ref 1033 2884 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051039 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "7c87cb24-e5e5-5da2-8a21-c94d9c1d1c5b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105541 #edb/ref 1069 "e-2947" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105541 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the area of the triangle $\\triangle ABC.$\n\n![](images/f8/f892cd084f548769fa6b7352fece0e1aef3a66a4ab517ed5b5243d89041ac3a9.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105541 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "fc5fc31e-f585-5d15-8a8a-eb0d19704e9a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105541 #edb/ref 1074 "The formula for the area of the triangle is\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{A} = \\dfrac{b \\cdot h}{2},\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $b$ is a base and $h$ is the corresponding height.\n\nFrom the diagram, we see that the base is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b & =AB \\\\  & =AH+HB \\\\  & =6+3 \\\\  & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the corresponding height is\n\n$$\nh={CH} = 4 .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{b\\cdot h}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{9\\cdot 4}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{36}{2} \\\\  & =18.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105542 #edb/ref 1069 "e-2949" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105542 #edb/ref 1071 "What is the area of the right triangle $ABC$ shown below?\n\n![](images/3b/3b68fc0b5c128122e7316db08e34932393db5bf557ffd889fd785265b758b447.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105542 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "cdb1d145-6e8a-5213-a7f7-8076440cbbeb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105542 #edb/ref 1074 "Since $m\\angle{A}=m\\angle{C},$ we have an isosceles triangle, where $AB=BC.$\n\nTherefore, the area of the triangle is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}AB\\cdot BC \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 8\\cdot 8 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {8}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 64 \\\\  & =32.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105543 #edb/ref 1069 "e-2950" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105543 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the triangles $\\triangle ABC$ and $\\triangle CDE$ shown below. If the area of $\\triangle CDE$ is $25 \\textrm{ cm}^2,$ find the area of $\\triangle ABC.$\n\n![](images/bd/bd9840fd674fe7bf7b4f428591dfc6e9fbbaec8387836ece34694d17eaabe65b.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105543 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "58e1ce31-f6fc-5900-ab89-e00241045b12" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105543 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's consider $\\triangle CDE.$ We can see that $\\overline{CD}$ is the height corresponding to the base $\\overline{DE}$ and that $DE=10\\textrm{ cm}.$ \n\nSince the area of $\\triangle CDE$ is $25\\textrm{ cm}^2,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{b\\cdot h}{2} \\\\ 25 & =\\frac{10\\cdot CD}{2} \\\\ 25 & =5\\cdot CD \\\\ CD & =5\\text{ cm}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that $\\overline{CD}$ is also the height of $\\triangle ABC$ corresponding to the base $\\overline{AB}.$\n\n![](images/ea/ea7f44b91e87a42029c5f00aae97a2eebf2e472ff890d4b91f4973e1e383e5c7.png)\n\nTherefore, we find the area of $\\triangle ABC$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{b\\cdot h}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{AB\\cdot CD}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{6\\cdot 5}{2} \\\\  & =15{\\text{ cm}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105870 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048472 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105870 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049608 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105870 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186106402 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105870 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186106403 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105870 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186253331 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105870 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186284686 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186105870 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 1759218633289