 17592186045417] [17592186508973 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508973 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/f2/f2c8ddcb1cddce59fbecf25ea606e8400d04d896dfdb263dd0aaf4e6c7de682f.png)\n\nThe area of the triangle $△ABC$ is{{field-1}}\n${cm}^{2}.$\n(Figure not drawn to scale)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508973 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c171c6db-2b08-53c8-ae94-2e1ffe80b16e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508973 #edb/ref 1074 "The area of the triangle is defined as \n\n$$\nA=\\frac{b\\cdot h}{2},\n$$\n\n where $b$ is a base and $h$ is the corresponding height.\n\nFrom the diagram, we see that the base is \n\n$$\nb=AB=12\\,\\text{cm}\n$$\n\nand the corresponding height is \n\n$$\nh=6\\,\\text{cm}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{b\\cdot h}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{12\\cdot 6}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{72}{2} \\\\  & =36\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508991 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186508897 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508991 #edb/ref 1069 "q-21055" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508991 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508991 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/b1/b12eaaaf9aafa591bef00ca1b11d24783fae4a46f7253bddb05b9b206dbcb9e6.png)\n\nFind $HB$ given that the area of $△ABC$ is $140\\,{m}^{2},$  $AH=15\\,m,$ and $CH=14\\,m.$\n\n(Figure not drawn to scale)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508991 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e5a09c22-28d0-55d4-9b16-abdb75945ec5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508991 #edb/ref 1074 "The area of the triangle is defined as \n\n$$\nA=\\frac{b\\cdot h}{2},\n$$\n\n where $b$ is a base and $h$ is the corresponding height.\n\nFrom the diagram, we see that the base is $b=AB,$ and the corresponding height is \n\n$$\nh=CH=14\\,m.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the formula for the area, we can solve for the base as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{b\\cdot h}{2} \\\\ 140 & =\\frac{b\\cdot 14}{2} \\\\ 280 & =b\\cdot 14 \\\\ b & =\\frac{280}{14} \\\\ b & =20\\,m\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, $AB=20\\,m$ and hence, we have\n\n$$\nHB=AB-AH=20-15=5\\,m.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508996 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186508876 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508996 #edb/ref 1069 "q-117368" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508996 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508996 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/b6/b66dea0994329cf63ff08137293efc33fe994a5ae0395d661dc014044a1c0d14.png)\n\nConsider the triangles $△ABC$ and $△CDE$ shown above. If the area of $△CDE$ is $27{\\text{ cm}}^{2}$, find the area of $△ABC.$\n\n(Figure not drawn to scale)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508996 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "efe752cf-6c60-5a23-aae8-55bc453cac9b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508996 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's consider $△CDE.$ We can see that $\\overset{―}{CD}$ is the height corresponding to the base $\\overset{―}{DE}$ and that $DE=6\\text{ cm}.$ \n\nSince the area of $△CDE$ is $27{\\text{ cm}}^{2},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{b\\cdot h}{2} \\\\ 27 & =\\frac{6\\cdot CD}{2} \\\\ 6CD & =54 \\\\ CD & =9\\text{ cm}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that $\\overset{―}{CD}$ is also the height of $△ABC$ corresponding to the base $\\overset{―}{AB}.$\n\n![](images/95/95e84873b1353e6a04bb4e6e7df5f45c09eb4751b1170d6eaf8a9bcfa4b572de.png)\n\nTherefore, we find the area of $△ABC$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{b\\cdot h}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{AB\\cdot CD}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\cdot 9}{2} \\\\  & =18{\\text{ cm}}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
